{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Design Draft and Theory-to-Prototype Alignment","title":"Accessibility and WCAG Checks","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting accessibility and WCAG checks to a WCAG 2.2 audit in which every change cites a success criterion and a reproducible result, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-01","statement":"Audit a screen against specific WCAG 2.2 AA success criteria, not general \"looks accessible\" impressions"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-02","statement":"Compute and check color contrast against the 4.5:1 threshold for normal text"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-03","statement":"Write alt text that conveys the information an image carries, not the word \"image.\""},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-04","statement":"Ensure no status or meaning is communicated by color alone"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment","accessibility","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"05","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/04/04"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_artifact_signal"],"ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_artifact_signal"],"ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Accessibility and WCAG Checks\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Accessibility, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/04-05-accessibility-and-wcag-checks.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Accessibility and WCAG Checks: Accessibility, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAccessibility is the place where \"looks fine to me\" is most often wrong, because the designer is rarely the person the design fails. A learner using a screen reader, a learner with low vision, and a learner who cannot distinguish red from green each experience a screen the sighted designer never sees. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines exist precisely to replace personal taste with verifiable criteria: a contrast ratio is a number you can compute, not an opinion. AIL 606 treats accessibility as a checkable revision, where every fix names the WCAG criterion it satisfies and can be confirmed by anyone, not asserted by the author.\n\nThis section teaches you to audit a draft screen against specific WCAG 2.2 AA success criteria (WCAG 2.2 is the current W3C Recommendation as of 2026; WCAG 3.0 remains a Working Draft, so audits should target 2.2 AA today) and to write fixes that are verifiable rather than tasteful. You should leave able to catch the three most common failures (insufficient contrast, useless alt text, and meaning carried by color alone) and to state which criterion each fix meets.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Audit a screen against specific WCAG 2.2 AA success criteria, not general \"looks accessible\" impressions.\n- Compute and check color contrast against the 4.5:1 threshold for normal text.\n- Write alt text that conveys the information an image carries, not the word \"image.\"\n- Ensure no status or meaning is communicated by color alone.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA quiz-feedback screen looks clean and professional. The instructions are in light gray, a workflow diagram sits at the top, and correct and incorrect answers are marked with a green check and a red cross. Three different learners hit three different walls: the low-vision learner cannot read gray text at roughly 2.8:1 contrast, the screen-reader user hears only \"image\" where the workflow should be described, and the color-blind learner sees two identical-looking marks because the only difference is hue. None of these learners is unusual, and none of them is served by a screen that \"looks fine.\"\n\nThe case shows why accessibility cannot be self-certified by the designer. Each failure is invisible to the person who built it and obvious the moment you apply the right criterion.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nWCAG is organized under four principles (perceivable, operable, understandable, robust) and expressed as testable success criteria at levels A, AA, and AAA. AA is the standard most institutions and Section 508 contexts require. The value of criteria over impressions is that each one is checkable by a third party. Three criteria account for the majority of instructional-media failures.\n\nContrast (criterion 1.4.3) requires a luminance contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 for normal text (3:1 for large text). The ratio is computed from the relative luminance of the two colors; gray text at #999 on white computes to about 2.8:1 and fails, while #333 on white clears the threshold. This is arithmetic, not preference. Text alternatives (criterion 1.1.1) require that non-text content carry an equivalent text alternative; alt=\"image\" satisfies the attribute but conveys nothing, so it fails the intent. Alt text must carry the information the image carries, which means a workflow diagram needs its steps and flow described, not its existence announced. Use of color (criterion 1.4.1) requires that color never be the only means of conveying information; a green check and red cross that differ only in hue fail, and pairing each with a word (Correct, Incorrect) or a distinct shape passes.\n\n| WCAG criterion | Failure to catch | Verifiable fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) | Body text below 4.5:1 | Darken text until the computed ratio clears 4.5:1 |\n| 1.1.1 Non-text Content | alt=\"image\" or empty alt on informative graphics | Describe what the image communicates |\n| 1.4.1 Use of Color | Status shown by color only | Add a word or shape alongside the color |\n\nThe reason these are revision checks and not aesthetic preferences is that each one yields a pass or fail that a reviewer can reproduce. \"I made it more accessible\" is an assertion; \"body text now computes at 4.6:1, clearing 1.4.3\" is evidence.\n\nContrast is arithmetic, not taste. Given the relative luminance of the text (\\$L_t\\$) and the background (\\$L_b\\$), the ratio is \\$(L_{light} + 0.05) / (L_{dark} + 0.05)\\$. With white text on a white-ish page the denominator stays near the background, so the ratio is \\$(L_b + 0.05)/(L_t + 0.05)\\$ for dark text on a light field. Drive the text luminance down and watch the ratio cross the 4.5:1 AA threshold (gray #999 sits near \\$L_t = 0.32\\$; #333 near \\$L_t = 0.03\\$).\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"WCAG 1.4.3 contrast ratio for dark text on a light background\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"Lb\",\"label\":\"Background relative luminance\",\"min\":0.5,\"max\":1,\"step\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":1},{\"name\":\"Lt\",\"label\":\"Text relative luminance\",\"min\":0,\"max\":0.6,\"step\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.32}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Contrast ratio (X to 1)\",\"unit\":\":1\",\"expr\":\"(Lb + 0.05) / (Lt + 0.05)\"},{\"label\":\"Margin over the 4.5 AA threshold\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"(Lb + 0.05) / (Lt + 0.05) - 4.5\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"Lt\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check question=\"An image of a three-step workflow has its alt attribute set to the bare word image. A validator reports no error. Why does this still fail WCAG 1.1.1?\" explanation=\"The validator only checks that the alt attribute exists, not that it carries the information in the image. A screen-reader user hears the word image and learns nothing about the workflow; the text alternative must convey the steps and flow the graphic communicates.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The alt attribute exists but carries none of the workflow information the image communicates.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The image file format is wrong for accessibility.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Alt text is never required on informative images.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It passes; a validator with no error means the screen is accessible.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWork through one annotated before/after accessibility revision of a single quiz-feedback screen against WCAG 2.2 AA (the three criteria used here, 1.4.3, 1.1.1, and 1.4.1, are unchanged from WCAG 2.1).\n\n**Before (feedback screen markup):**\n\n```html\n<p style=\"color:#999\">Read each item and choose the best answer.</p>\n<img src=\"workflow.png\" alt=\"image\">\n<span style=\"color:green\">&#10003;</span>  <!-- correct -->\n<span style=\"color:red\">&#10007;</span>    <!-- incorrect -->\n```\n\n**Annotations a reviewer would leave:**\n\n1. Body text at #999 on white is roughly 2.8:1 contrast, below the 4.5:1 AA threshold.\n2. The diagram's alt text says \"image,\" which conveys none of the workflow it depicts.\n3. Correct and incorrect differ only by color (green vs red), so a color-blind learner cannot tell the status apart.\n\n**After (revised markup):**\n\n```html\n<p style=\"color:#333\">Read each item and choose the best answer.</p>\n<img src=\"workflow.png\"\n     alt=\"Three-step intake workflow: request, review, then approve, with review looping back on rejection\">\n<span style=\"color:#1a7f37\">&#10003; Correct</span>\n<span style=\"color:#b42318\">&#10007; Incorrect</span>\n```\n\n**Why the after version is stronger:** the body color #333 on white clears 4.5:1, the alt text now carries the workflow's actual information for screen-reader users, and each status pairs its color with a word so meaning no longer depends on color discrimination. Each fix is checked against a specific WCAG criterion, so the revision is verifiable, not a matter of taste.\n\nMatch each common failure to the named WCAG criterion it violates and the verifiable fix that resolves it. Naming the criterion is what turns \"looks fine\" into reproducible evidence.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Body text at #999 on white, about 2.8 to 1\",\"definition\":\"1.4.3 Contrast: darken the text until the computed ratio clears 4.5 to 1\"},{\"term\":\"A workflow diagram whose alt text is just the word image\",\"definition\":\"1.1.1 Non-text Content: describe the steps and flow the diagram communicates\"},{\"term\":\"Correct and incorrect shown only by green vs red\",\"definition\":\"1.4.1 Use of Color: pair each status with a word or distinct shape\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nReplace \"looks accessible\" with a criterion and a result. For every accessibility revision:\n\n1. **Criterion check:** Which named WCAG success criterion does this fix address?\n2. **Measurement check:** Can a reviewer reproduce the result (compute the contrast, read the alt text, confirm a non-color cue)?\n3. **Coverage check:** Did you test for the failures invisible to you, not just the ones you happened to notice?\n\nIf a fix cannot be stated as a criterion plus a reproducible result, it is decoration, not an accessibility revision.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first error is self-certification: declaring a screen accessible because it looks readable to the designer, who is exactly the wrong judge. The second is treating alt text as a label to satisfy a validator (alt=\"image\") rather than an information equivalent for someone who cannot see the graphic. The third is assuming bright, saturated color is sufficient signaling, when color alone fails any learner who cannot distinguish the hues.\n\nAccessibility is conformance to verifiable criteria for the people the design excludes, not a sighted designer's impression of readability.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nStart by listing who the design might exclude: low vision, no vision, color vision deficiency, motor constraints. Each audience maps to specific criteria, which converts a vague goal (\"be accessible\") into a finite checklist you can actually run.\n\nThen test, do not eyeball. Run text colors through a contrast formula or checker against 4.5:1. Read every informative image's alt text aloud and ask whether a person who cannot see it would now know what it shows. View the screen in grayscale to expose any meaning that lives only in hue. Testing is what turns an impression into evidence.\n\nFinally, record each fix as criterion plus result. \"Darkened body text to #333, computed 5.7:1, satisfies 1.4.3\" is an audit trail a reviewer can verify, and it is the form your disclosure and traceability artifacts will reuse. Accessibility done this way produces evidence, not just compliance.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence** on the right. The claim is the WCAG criterion you believe a feature violates or satisfies; the evidence is the measured result (the ratio, the alt-text content, the non-color cue). By the end of AIL 606 04.05, your notes should let a reviewer reproduce every accessibility judgment.\n\nA strong reader of an accessibility pass can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which named criterion does each fix satisfy?\n2. What is the measured contrast ratio, and does it clear 4.5:1?\n3. Does the alt text carry the image's information or just its existence?\n4. Which failures were invisible to the designer until tested?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when every accessibility change cites a WCAG criterion and a reproducible result, and when the audit covered the failures the designer could not personally perceive. Treat \"looks fine\" as an unverified claim. Where you are unsure a fix conforms, mark it with the annotation tools and ask BigAL to compare a failing example against a passing one for that exact criterion rather than for general accessibility advice.\n\nA useful final paragraph for the artifact would sound like this: \"I darkened the body text to clear 1.4.3 at 5.7:1, rewrote the diagram alt text to describe the workflow for 1.1.1, and added Correct and Incorrect labels for 1.4.1. I rejected the suggestion to brighten the green check, because color alone would still fail color-blind learners.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same screen from a desktop web prototype to a printed, black-and-white job aid. Contrast and color-only concerns change shape: the green and red marks become indistinguishable grays in print, so the word labels you added now carry the entire status, and the alt-text criterion no longer applies but a figure caption must do the same information work. Transfer means re-deriving which criteria govern the new medium, not copying the HTML fixes. If you understand WCAG, changing the medium changes which criteria are even in play while the underlying obligation (perceivable to everyone) persists.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the WCAG accessibility audit packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/04-05-accessibility-and-wcag-checks-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"WCAG 2.2 accessibility audit with per-criterion findings and reproducible fixes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"04.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the WCAG accessibility audit.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the WCAG accessibility audit for AIL 606 04.05. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_accessibility_and_wcag_checks\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"accessibility\", \"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm that each accessibility fix names a WCAG criterion and a reproducible result, and that you tested for failures you could not personally perceive. That verifiability is what separates an accessibility pass from a designer's impression.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"accessibility","stem":"A draft screen uses light gray text (#999999) on a white background for body copy. Against WCAG 2.2 AA, which is the correct judgment?","options":["It passes, because gray on white is a common, clean look.","It fails AA: body text needs a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1, and #999 on white is about 2.8:1, so the text must be darkened.","It passes, because contrast rules apply only to images.","Contrast cannot be evaluated until the final colors are chosen."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"WCAG 2.2 AA (unchanged from 2.1 on this criterion) requires 4.5:1 contrast for normal-size body text. #999 on white falls well short, so this is a measurable failure, not a style preference.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_a11y_contrast_as_taste","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["accessibility"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-01","ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"accessibility","stem":"An image of a workflow diagram carries alt text reading \"image.\" Why does this fail an accessibility check even though alt text is present?","options":["Alt text must always be at least one sentence long.","Alt text must convey the information the image carries; \"image\" tells a screen-reader user nothing about the workflow, so the content is still inaccessible.","Decorative images are not allowed to have alt text.","The check fails only if the alt text is missing entirely."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Present-but-useless alt text is a common failure. Alt text must describe the informational content for users who cannot see the image, not merely exist.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_a11y_alt_text_presence_only","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["accessibility"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to generate alt text for all 12 images in your prototype. It returns fluent descriptions. What is the strongest bounded use?","options":["Paste all 12 in, since the descriptions read well.","Check each against what the image must communicate in context, since AI alt text often describes appearance but misses the instructional point the image serves.","Ask the AI to also decide which images are decorative and skip those.","Trust the AI for charts but write your own for photos."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI alt text describes what it sees, not why the image is on the page. The designer must confirm each description conveys the image's role in the lesson.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_a11y_ai_alt_text_unchecked","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_accessibility_and_wcag_checks_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Your draft signals correct answers using a green checkmark and incorrect ones using a red X, with no other difference. Which WCAG concern does this raise, and what is the defensible fix?","options":["No concern; red and green are standard for right and wrong.","It relies on color alone to convey meaning, which fails users with color vision deficiency; add a text or shape label so the status is not carried by color alone.","The concern is only that green and red clash visually.","The fix is to make the checkmark and X larger."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"WCAG requires that color not be the only means of conveying information. Pairing the color with text or a distinct shape makes the status perceivable without color discrimination.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_a11y_color_only_meaning","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-05-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_a11y_contrast_as_taste","pattern":"contrast_treated_as_style","description":"Treating text contrast as an aesthetic preference rather than a measurable WCAG threshold","trigger":"learner defends low-contrast text as a clean look without checking the ratio","feedback":"Measure the contrast ratio against the AA threshold (4.5:1 for body text). Contrast is a pass or fail check, not a taste call.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_a11y_alt_text_presence_only","pattern":"alt_text_presence_mistaken_for_quality","description":"Assuming an image is accessible because any alt text exists, regardless of whether it conveys the content","trigger":"alt text is generic such as image or photo and carries none of the informational content","feedback":"Write alt text that conveys the information the image carries in context. Presence is not the same as a usable description.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_a11y_ai_alt_text_unchecked","pattern":"ai_alt_text_accepted_unchecked","description":"Accepting AI-generated alt text that describes appearance but misses the image's instructional role","trigger":"learner pastes AI alt text without checking it against why the image is on the page","feedback":"Check each AI description against what the image must communicate in the lesson. Appearance is not the same as instructional meaning.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_5_a11y_color_only_meaning","pattern":"meaning_carried_by_color_alone","description":"Using color as the only channel for status or meaning, excluding users with color vision deficiency","trigger":"correct and incorrect or other states differ only by color","feedback":"Pair color with text or a distinct shape so the meaning is perceivable without color discrimination.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"c7301336f95d4d638ce56dadde5f39c21852994b1a7c374af2f28e28ae5a20a7","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}